 "result_text": "Green now reports a `SyntaxError` when you name a test module explicitly. Before, it printed \"No Tests Found\".\n\nI reproduced it with a module containing `X X`. `python -m green tests.test_my` now shows the full traceback, ending in `SyntaxError: invalid syntax`. I didn't read the rest of the output, so I can't say how the failure is labelled in the summary.\n\nThe cause was in `loadTarget` in `green/loader.py`. The \"dotted object\" step swallowed every exception from the import, so a syntax error looked the same as a missing module. I changed it to remember the `SyntaxError` and return it as a failing test if no other loading method finds anything. Other import errors are still ignored as before. I also replaced the loop that deleted `_FailedTest` entries while iterating over them with a list filter.\n\nGreen's own suite gives the same result with and without my change (232 tests, 25 errors), so those 25 failures were already there. I didn't add a test, as you asked.",
